**(i) Diagram (to be drawn by the candidate).** A vertical section of the heart showing **four chambers**: the **right atrium** and **right ventricle** on the left of the drawing, the **left atrium** and **left ventricle** on the right (the left ventricle has the thicker wall), separated by the **septum**. Label also:

- the **vena cava** (bringing deoxygenated blood from the body into the right atrium);
- the **tricuspid valve** (between right atrium and right ventricle) and the **bicuspid (mitral) valve** (between left atrium and left ventricle);
- the **pulmonary artery** (carrying deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs) and the **pulmonary vein** (bringing oxygenated blood from the lungs into the left atrium);
- the **aorta** (carrying oxygenated blood from the left ventricle to the body), with the **semilunar valves** at the exits of the ventricles.

**(ii) Three functions of the heart.**

1. It **pumps oxygenated blood** from the left ventricle through the aorta to all the tissues of the body, supplying oxygen and nutrients.
2. It **pumps deoxygenated blood** from the right ventricle to the lungs through the pulmonary artery, where carbon dioxide is released and oxygen is taken up.
3. It **maintains blood pressure and a continuous one-way circulation** (the valves prevent backflow), so that waste products such as carbon dioxide are carried away from the tissues; it keeps the oxygenated and deoxygenated blood separate.
